    "long_instructions": "Misconceptions and what to test for:\r\n\r\n- This is the \"partly mine\" level. Any sensor and any action is fine, as long as the program runs and has an if with a check, an else, and a purpose comment above the if.\r\n- At the start, can_move() is True, and is_on_bucket(), has_paint(), and is_on_paint() are False. So the branch that runs depends on the check a student chooses. Ask: \"Which branch ran, and how do you know?\" Then ask them to make the other branch run.\r\n- Moving onto the bucket (one move) makes is_on_bucket() True. Turning left once from the start faces the edge of the map, so can_move() is False.\r\n- Hazards: moving into the building (a fifth move east) or off the map crashes with INVALID_MOVE. Calling paint() while standing on the bucket with paint in hand crashes with \"Painter tried to paint off the grid or over an obstacle.\" That message is misleading, because the cause is the bucket. Point students to it.\r\n- A comment that only repeats the code (\"if on bucket\") does not explain why. Ask: \"What is the Painter checking for, and why?\"\r\n- Else never takes a condition. Forgetting the colon or the indent is the most common error.\r\n- can_move(\"east\") and similar directions also work. Accept them.\r\n- Validate checks the structure, the comment, and that the Painter's final state changed. It cannot tell whether both branches ran or whether a crash happened after the else. Ask students to show you both branches running.\r\n- If a student uses Build Python, they still own the program. Ask them to explain which branch ran and why.\r\n\r\nValidation checks that the program has an if with can_move(), is_on_bucket(), has_paint(), or is_on_paint(), uses an else, has a purpose comment above the if, and ends in a different state than it started.",
